====================================================================
                    AWS VPC PEERING
====================================================================

1. WHAT IS VPC PEERING?
====================================================================

VPC Peering is a networking connection between two VPCs.

It allows resources in one VPC to communicate with resources
in another VPC using PRIVATE IP addresses.

Simple:

VPC-A  ------------------  VPC-B
         VPC Peering

Example:

VPC-A                         VPC-B
10.0.0.0/16                  20.0.0.0/16

EC2-A                         EC2-B
10.0.1.10  ----------------> 20.0.1.10
              Private IP


====================================================================
2. WHY DO WE USE VPC PEERING?
====================================================================

We use VPC Peering when two VPCs need to communicate privately.

Example:

Company has:

VPC-A = Application VPC
VPC-B = Database VPC

Application server is in VPC-A.

Database server is in VPC-B.

We need:

Application VPC
      |
      | VPC Peering
      |
Database VPC

So the application can communicate with the database
using private IP addresses.


====================================================================
3. REAL-TIME DEVOPS EXAMPLE
====================================================================

Suppose:

VPC-A
CIDR = 10.0.0.0/16

Application EC2:
10.0.1.10


VPC-B
CIDR = 20.0.0.0/16

Database EC2:
20.0.1.20

Application wants to connect to MySQL:

10.0.1.10  ------------>  20.0.1.20:3306
              Peering

This communication can happen through VPC Peering.


====================================================================
4. IMPORTANT REQUIREMENT - CIDR MUST NOT OVERLAP
====================================================================

This is VERY IMPORTANT.

Correct:

VPC-A = 10.0.0.0/16
VPC-B = 20.0.0.0/16

Different CIDRs = GOOD


Incorrect:

VPC-A = 10.0.0.0/16
VPC-B = 10.0.0.0/16

Overlapping CIDRs = NOT allowed for peering.


Easy memory:

Different VPCs
      +
Different CIDRs
      =
Peering possible


====================================================================
5. HOW VPC PEERING WORKS
====================================================================

There are 3 main steps:

STEP 1:
Create VPC Peering Connection

STEP 2:
Accept the Peering Connection

STEP 3:
Update Route Tables


Flow:

VPC-A
  |
  | Create Peering
  |
  +----------------+
                   |
              VPC Peering
                   |
  +----------------+
  |
VPC-B


====================================================================
6. ROUTE TABLE - VERY IMPORTANT
====================================================================

Creating VPC Peering does NOT automatically configure routing.

We need to add routes.

Example:

VPC-A:

CIDR:
10.0.0.0/16

VPC-B:

CIDR:
20.0.0.0/16


VPC-A Route Table:

Destination        Target
20.0.0.0/16        pcx-12345


VPC-B Route Table:

Destination        Target
10.0.0.0/16        pcx-12345


pcx-12345 = VPC Peering Connection ID


Easy memory:

PEERING = Connection

ROUTE TABLE = Direction


====================================================================
7. SECURITY GROUP
====================================================================

Route table allows the network path.

But Security Group must also allow the traffic.

Example:

Application:

10.0.1.10

Database:

20.0.1.20


Database Security Group:

Type       : MySQL
Port       : 3306
Source     : 10.0.0.0/16


Then:

10.0.1.10
     |
     | TCP 3306
     |
     v
20.0.1.20


Remember:

VPC Peering
     +
Route Table
     +
Security Group
     =
Communication


====================================================================
8. NETWORK ACL
====================================================================

Network ACL can also affect communication.

If communication is not working, check:

1. Security Group
2. Network ACL
3. Route Table


Troubleshooting:

EC2-A  --------X-------->  EC2-B

Check:

Peering status
     |
Route table
     |
Security Group
     |
Network ACL
     |
Application port


====================================================================
9. VPC PEERING IS PRIVATE
====================================================================

VPC Peering allows private communication between VPCs.

Example:

VPC-A                         VPC-B

10.0.1.10  ----------------> 20.0.1.20
             Private


We don't need to expose the instances through public IPs
just for VPC-to-VPC communication.


====================================================================
10. SAME REGION
====================================================================

VPC Peering can connect VPCs in the same AWS Region.

Example:

AWS Region: ap-south-1

VPC-A  ----------------  VPC-B
         Peering


====================================================================
11. DIFFERENT REGIONS
====================================================================

VPC Peering can also connect VPCs in different AWS Regions.

This is called:

INTER-REGION VPC PEERING


Example:

ap-south-1                  us-east-1

VPC-A  --------------------  VPC-B
         Peering


====================================================================
12. DIFFERENT AWS ACCOUNTS
====================================================================

VPC Peering can also be used between VPCs
belonging to different AWS accounts.

Example:

AWS Account-A              AWS Account-B

VPC-A  ------------------  VPC-B
          Peering


The peering request needs to be accepted by
the other side as applicable.


====================================================================
13. VPC PEERING IS NOT TRANSITIVE
====================================================================

VERY IMPORTANT INTERVIEW QUESTION.


Suppose:

VPC-A  <------>  VPC-B
                    ^
                    |
                    |
                 VPC-C


OR:

VPC-A <----> VPC-B <----> VPC-C


Can VPC-A automatically communicate with VPC-C?

ANSWER:

NO ❌

VPC Peering is NOT TRANSITIVE.


Example:

A ---- B ---- C

A cannot automatically use B as a router
to reach C.


Easy memory:

VPC PEERING = DIRECT CONNECTION

NOT TRANSITIVE


====================================================================
14. VPC PEERING VS INTERNET GATEWAY
====================================================================

INTERNET GATEWAY:

Used for:

VPC <----> Internet


Example:

EC2
 |
Internet Gateway
 |
Internet


VPC PEERING:

Used for:

VPC <----> VPC


Easy memory:

Internet Gateway = Internet

VPC Peering = Another VPC


====================================================================
15. VPC PEERING VS NAT GATEWAY
====================================================================

NAT Gateway:

Private Subnet
      |
      v
NAT Gateway
      |
      v
Internet


VPC Peering:

VPC-A
  |
  v
VPC Peering
  |
  v
VPC-B


Easy memory:

NAT Gateway = Private subnet to Internet

VPC Peering = VPC to VPC


====================================================================
16. VPC PEERING VS TRANSIT GATEWAY
====================================================================

If we have only a few VPCs:

VPC-A -------- VPC-B

VPC Peering can be simple.


If we have many VPCs:

VPC-A
   |
VPC-B
   |
VPC-C
   |
VPC-D
   |
VPC-E


Managing many peering connections can become complicated.


Transit Gateway provides a centralized design:

             Transit Gateway
            /      |      \
           /       |       \
        VPC-A    VPC-B    VPC-C


Easy memory:

Few VPCs  --> VPC Peering

Many VPCs --> Consider Transit Gateway


